<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael, Row the Boat Ashore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">noted <a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1863">1863</a>; first published <a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Michael, Row the Boat Ashore&quot; is a traditional spiritual first noted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> on St. Helena Island, one of the Sea Islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. It was sung by formerly enslaved people whose owners had abandoned the island before the Union navy arrived to enforce its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Charles Pickard Ware, an abolitionist and Harvard graduate who supervised plantations on St. Helena Island from 1862 to 1865, wrote the song down in music notation as he heard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> sing it. His cousin William Francis Allen reported in 1863 that the rowers sang it as they took him by boat across Station Creek. Allen, Ware and Lucy McKim Garrison printed it in Slave Songs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1867. According to Allen, the Michael of the song is the Archangel Michael, in Catholic tradition a guide of the souls of the dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>One of the oldest printed versions runs as a series of unrhymed couplets answered by &quot;Hallelujah!&quot;, and the same source gives a second, rhymed version about the sounding of the trumpet. Its many oral versions tell of crossing the River Jordan, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was baptised: a figure of deliverance and salvation, but also of the boundary of the Promised Land and of death and the passage to Heaven. Guy and Candie Carawan recorded a traditional &quot;Row, Michael, Row&quot; on Johns Island in the 1960s; the singer Janie Hunter remembered her father, the son of former slaves, singing it as he rowed home after fishing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> folksinger and teacher Tony Saletan found the song in a library copy of the 1867 book in 1954, added a guitar accompaniment and replaced the repeated single lines with rhyming traditional couplets for the teenagers of Shaker Village Work Camp. He taught it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>, and the Weavers sang it at their reunion concert on Christmas Eve 1955. The Highwaymen's recording, released in 1960 as &quot;Michael&quot;, reached number one on Billboard's Hot 100 for two weeks in September 1961 and also topped the chart in the United Kingdom; it changed &quot;meet my mother on the other side&quot; to &quot;milk and honey on the other side&quot;.</p><p>The same month, civil rights activists jailed in Amite County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, after the murder of Herbert Lee sang a version led by Hollis Watkins, ending &quot;Mississippi's next to go&quot;. Harry Belafonte, Lonnie Donegan, Trini Lopez and many others recorded it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael, Row the Boat Ashore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
